<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE score-partwise PUBLIC "-//Recordare//DTD MusicXML 4.0 Partwise//EN" "http://www.musicxml.org/dtds/partwise.dtd">
<!--
  A score with enough on it to photograph the engraved page.

  Hand-written, like the two fixtures beside it, and for the same reason: this
  is the input to a reference image. What makes it different from
  clean-import.musicxml is that it is about the *page* rather than the import
  summary, so it is long enough to break into several systems and carries one
  of everything the renderer draws.

  What a reviewer should be able to find in a picture of it:

    - a key signature of two sharps at the head of every staff of every system
    - a 3/4 time signature
    - a braced grand staff under a single-staff part
    - chords of three and four notes, stacked low to high
    - an accidental printed only where the key does not already supply it:
      the E natural in bar 7 and the A natural in bar 10 are printed, and the
      F sharps and E flats in the key are not
    - ledger lines above the treble staff (bar 4) and below the bass (bars 10
      and 12)
    - a clef change on the piano's lower staff alone, at bar 7, leaving the
      upper staff's placement untouched: the notes after it move, and on a
      system that *starts* at or after bar 7 the new clef is drawn at the head
    - rests of three durations
    - the same onset at the same x on every staff
    - a stem on every note shorter than a whole, one per chord and never one
      per note, pointing down above the middle line and up below it
    - bar 3: two eighths beamed as a pair, four sixteenths beamed with a second
      beam under the first, and a dotted eighth and sixteenth whose sixteenth
      carries a short stub rather than a full second beam
    - a dotted half filling bar 11, with its dot in the space beside the
      notehead rather than on the line through it
    - 3/4, so the beams group in quarters: three pairs rather than one run of
      six

  It deliberately contains nothing the renderer cannot draw yet: no dots, no
  ties, no slurs, no dynamics, no tempo mark, no lyrics, no repeats. When the
  renderer learns one of those, this fixture is where it should be added, and
  the list above is what the surface's questions are drawn from.

  One thing it does contain that the renderer does not yet draw, kept on
  purpose because it is what the fixture found: the key change at bar 5 and the
  clef change at bar 7 are drawn only when a system happens to start at or
  after them. A signature change in the middle of a system is not drawn at all,
  so on a wide enough window the staff keeps showing two sharps through music
  written in three flats. That is a misreading rather than a missing
  refinement, and it has no render diagnostic either, which the renderer's own
  rule says it should. Finding 10.

  It is in the surface's questions, so a reviewer records it rather than
  rediscovering it.

  Twelve bars, 3/4, divisions of 4: every measure is exactly 12 and no
  invariant has anything to repair.
-->
